  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The cold front is making its way south across\nthe CWA and is currently draped roughly parallel to Highway 264.\nThis boundary, along with any sea/river/sound breezes today will\nbe the forcing mechanisms at play for shower and thunderstorm\ndevelopment. The forecast has trended down in terms of coverage,\nbut the environment is still supportive of an isolated strong to\nmarginally severe thunderstorm capable of producing gusty winds\nand intense rainfall rates. Latest mesoanalysis shows 40-45 kt \nof bulk shear across the area with 1.5-2\" PWATs and 1000-1500 \nJ/kg MLCAPE roughly south of Highway 70. This will be the area \nmost likely to see convective activity this afternoon and into \nearly this evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The cold front will clear the CWA tonight,\nleaving cooler and drier conditions in its wake on Friday as\nnortheast flow develops. The front will then lift back north\nas a warm front on Saturday. This will bring our next round of\nshowers and thunderstorms with the best chances being south of\nHighway 264 (30-60%).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A reinforcing cold front will move through on\nSaturday night and usher in even drier and cooler air to finish\nout the weekend. Lows will drop to the mid-50s/low-60s Saturday\nnight with highs in the 70s on Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...High pressure will shift offshore on Monday with\nthe next front forecast to cross the area late Monday/early\nTuesday. Showers and thunderstorms will be possible Monday-\nTuesday with chances peaking Monday night near the coast\n(40-60%).",
